For my own special dinner party, I would choose a set of figures who were all great communicators and writers - a dinner party that celebrates the power of the written word, and also allows me to learn about the history of rhetoric and oratory.

I would invite:

1. Martin Luther King Jr., as the guest speaker for this dinner party;

2. Winston Churchill, the prime minister of the UK during World War II, whose speeches helped inspire his countrymen through a difficult time;

3. Abraham Lincoln, president of the United States who delivered many famous speeches and letters, including the Gettysburg Address and his Second Inaugural Address;

4. Edward R. Murrow, an American broadcaster and journalist known for his courageous and insightful reporting on political events in the 1950s and beyond;

5. Socrates, the Greek philosopher whose teaching style was characterized by asking questions and inspiring thought.
